From 01b947aa92aa937622194942fa598fcafbeaccb3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Project Luma <engineering@projectluma.org>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 23:04:26 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] wayland: Let a client keep its window at an aspect ratio

X11 clients keep their content's shape through the ICCCM aspect hints in
WM_NORMAL_HINTS, which constrain_aspect_ratio() applies to every size
Mutter picks, including each step of an interactive resize. xdg-shell has
no such hint, so a Wayland client (an emulator, a video player) can only
fit its window after a resize ends, and the window takes any shape while
it is dragged.

luma_window_shape_v1 adds one. get_window_shape() takes the wl_surface
rather than the xdg_toplevel, because GTK keeps its xdg_toplevel private;
the state applies while the surface is an xdg_toplevel. set_aspect_ratio
(width, height, base_width, base_height) and unset_aspect_ratio are
double-buffered and reach the window's size hints on the next commit,
through the xdg_toplevel role. The ratio is kept in min_aspect/max_aspect,
so constrain_aspect_ratio() applies it while the window floats, but it is
measured on the frame rectangle (the window geometry) less the base, the
client's fixed chrome such as a header bar, rather than on the client
rectangle, which for a Wayland client includes the invisible borders.

A maximized window with a ratio becomes the largest size with that ratio
inside the area it would otherwise fill (including the shell's maximize
insets), centred. Windows tiled side by side, or marked tiled by a tiling
manager, fill their tile: a tile's size belongs to the layout, and the
client letterboxes. Fullscreen windows are not affected. The work-area
fit shrinks such a window in proportion instead of one side alone.

A tiling manager's tile now also takes precedence over X11 aspect hints,
as Mutter's own side-by-side tiles already did.
